WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:03.617
"In war towns all over the United States

00:00:03.617 --> 00:00:07.250
women are called upon to leave 
their homes and take jobs."

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:12.432
During World War II, nearly 1 in 3 
American men went off to war.

00:00:12.432 --> 00:00:16.048
And women were expected to 
take their place at work.

00:00:16.048 --> 00:00:19.872
"They discovered that factory work is usually
no more difficult than house work."

00:00:19.872 --> 00:00:21.110
"Isn't this pretty hot for you?"

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:24.539
"Well I hear it gets kind of hot 
around a kitchen stove, too."

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:26.738
But the problem was...

00:00:26.738 --> 00:00:30.695
who was going to watch 
Rosie the Riveter’s kids?

00:00:30.695 --> 00:00:34.007
“When married women with small children
have to take jobs

00:00:34.007 --> 00:00:38.430
everything possible will be done 
to provide day-care for the children.”

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.794
For 50 cents a day, which would be 
about $8 now

00:00:41.794 --> 00:00:45.769
mothers could leave their kids at 
a government-funded day care center.

00:00:45.769 --> 00:00:47.761
Lunch was included.

00:00:47.761 --> 00:00:52.055
America actually set up universal child care
for working mothers.

00:00:52.055 --> 00:00:55.000
Canada and other countries did too.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.382
Around half a million American children 
attended these centers.

00:01:00.796 --> 00:01:02.200
But when the war ended...

00:01:02.393 --> 00:01:04.622
"Joy is unconfined."

00:01:06.344 --> 00:01:07.737
...so did the daycare.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.591
Families protested, calling for 
the centers to be permanent.

00:01:12.591 --> 00:01:16.158
But the federal funding stopped in 1946.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.622
And so the US missed that start 

00:01:21.622 --> 00:01:25.070
to begin a climb towards 
other child care policies.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.525
Other countries didn’t.

00:01:27.525 --> 00:01:29.597
Canada had the same protests.

00:01:29.597 --> 00:01:33.099
And some provinces decided to keep
their centers open.

00:01:33.099 --> 00:01:35.097
That decision started them down a path 

00:01:35.097 --> 00:01:39.180
where it was easy to implement 
other government-run policies.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.270
So, how did the US end up here?

00:01:48.981 --> 00:01:51.366
Here are some of the 
richest countries in the world.

00:01:51.366 --> 00:01:56.252
And here’s when each of them established
a paid maternity leave policy.

00:01:56.252 --> 00:01:59.099
And a universal child care policy.

00:01:59.099 --> 00:02:02.463
And let’s throw in paid paternity leave
for good measure.

00:02:02.463 --> 00:02:07.419
In France, they’ve actually had some form
of universal child care since the 1800s.

00:02:07.419 --> 00:02:10.450
But formalized the system in 1945.

00:02:11.383 --> 00:02:14.751
In Sweden, considered to have one of
the best systems in the world,

00:02:14.751 --> 00:02:17.977
they were the first ones to guarantee 
paid paternity leave.

00:02:17.977 --> 00:02:23.025
And they’ve provided full child care 
for all children since 1985.

00:02:23.025 --> 00:02:27.170
You’ll notice the US has none of these policies.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.284
Technically there is a 12-week 
maternity leave policy.

00:02:30.284 --> 00:02:31.739
It’s just not paid.

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:33.709
That’s up to your employer.

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:38.400
And a few states offer preschool and 
there are some child care programs

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.420
but they’re mostly for low-income families
and far from universal.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.510
Child care in the US right now 
is very fragmented

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.787
There are different programs that 
function for different purposes 

00:02:50.787 --> 00:02:52.934
and often work against each other.

00:02:52.934 --> 00:02:54.612
Take a closer look at this chart.

00:02:54.612 --> 00:02:58.559
After WWII was a popular time for 
these child care policies 

00:02:58.559 --> 00:03:00.924
but so was the 70s.

00:03:04.803 --> 00:03:07.849
The early 70s were a time for women’s rights.

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:09.872
Equal employment laws had just passed.

00:03:09.872 --> 00:03:11.640
Divorce laws were loosened.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.664
And many countries established 
universal child care policies.

00:03:15.664 --> 00:03:18.289
It almost happened in the US, too.

00:03:18.289 --> 00:03:19.080
Almost.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.090
What happened in 1971 is that

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:25.689
there was obviously a sense that we needed
to do something about child care.

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:28.700
And Congress actually acted.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:32.879
Both the House and Senate passed 
the Comprehensive Child Development Act.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:34.459
This was a really extraordinary bill

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:37.053
because it would have provided 
a universal child care.

00:03:37.053 --> 00:03:40.159
It would not have been stigmatized 
in the same way 

00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:41.803
that welfare programs would have been.

00:03:41.803 --> 00:03:46.029
It would have been available 
to a much wider group of families.

00:03:46.029 --> 00:03:50.922
But then President Richard Nixon vetoed it.

00:03:50.922 --> 00:03:52.787
No one expected the veto.

00:03:52.787 --> 00:03:54.620
It was a terrible shock.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:59.684
His veto message said that this is going to
create communal child care rearing.

00:03:59.684 --> 00:04:03.049
It would Sovietize the American family.

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:07.302
That language was a response to criticism
that he was going soft on communism

00:04:07.302 --> 00:04:09.457
like by visiting China.

00:04:09.457 --> 00:04:10.696
But really...

00:04:10.696 --> 00:04:12.934
The right wing began to see this

00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:16.450
as an encroachment because
it could be used

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:19.900
potentially by white, middle-class families

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:24.379
in order to support women leaving the home
and going out into the workforce.

00:04:24.379 --> 00:04:26.330
They're not supposed to be working.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:32.211
We do want to make, you know poor, black, and
brown indigenous mothers

00:04:32.211 --> 00:04:36.940
like those are the non-deserving kinds of
mothers that shouldn't be on welfare.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:38.467
They should be working.

00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:41.243
Conservative Phyllis Schlafly led that opposition.

00:04:41.243 --> 00:04:46.410
"Women whose husbands have left them or divorced
them or whatever, have a very hard time."

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:50.533
"But when you look at these wives who 

00:04:50.533 --> 00:04:55.000
simply want a standard of living higher than
their husband is producing

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.490
crying around that they want somebody else 
to pay for their daycare."

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:03.408
They were organizing against child care because
it would take mothers out of the home.

00:05:03.408 --> 00:05:07.874
Just as the European countries were starting
their child care systems

00:05:07.874 --> 00:05:09.679
we were shutting the door.

00:05:09.679 --> 00:05:13.199
The day after Nixon vetoes the CDA

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:18.389
he signed into law a bill that created 
child care tax deductions

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:20.858
for middle class and affluent families.

00:05:20.858 --> 00:05:24.120
This creates a two tiered system,

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.560
on one hand, you have these tax supports.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.350
And then on the other hand, you get these
much more stigmatized

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:35.542
direct supports for only low-income families.

00:05:35.542 --> 00:05:41.948
Meanwhile these countries were able to follow
a path from one universal policy to another.

00:05:41.948 --> 00:05:44.419
Experts call this “path dependency.”

00:05:44.419 --> 00:05:48.389
So for these countries, once they established
one universal policy

00:05:48.389 --> 00:05:51.750
creating others was fairly straightforward.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:56.031
Where in the US, it’s been easier to design
policies within the parameters

00:05:56.031 --> 00:05:58.500
 of the existing income-based programs

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.917
or tax breaks that Nixon founded.

00:06:00.917 --> 00:06:02.910
He set a path where 

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:10.493
any kind of federal child care policy would be as 
underfunded, stigmatized, as possible.

00:06:10.493 --> 00:06:18.173
It's a system that reinforces and deepens
inequalities of race, gender, and class.

00:06:18.173 --> 00:06:23.009
It doesn't end up helping 
so many of our families.

00:06:23.009 --> 00:06:26.957
But just because we’re on that path 
doesn’t mean we can’t change.

00:06:26.957 --> 00:06:28.993
Just look at the United Kingdom.

00:06:28.993 --> 00:06:33.733
From a birds eye view these, countries 
are following very similar paths.

00:06:33.733 --> 00:06:36.949
Neither continued the day care centers 
after WWII.

00:06:36.949 --> 00:06:40.000
Both had attempted child care policy 
in the 70s.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.232
The UK’s failed attempt was actually led
by conservative Margaret Thatcher.

00:06:44.232 --> 00:06:50.480
But in the 90s, the UK actually shifted and
established a universal pre-k program.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.302
So, what changed?

00:06:52.302 --> 00:06:55.713
It's framed as education, not welfare.

00:06:55.713 --> 00:06:58.151
So those questions of deservingness

00:06:58.151 --> 00:07:03.177
aren't applicable in the same way because
it's education for all children.

00:07:03.177 --> 00:07:08.596
It's part-time, it's not as generous,
but I think that it is

00:07:08.596 --> 00:07:12.782
an example of how you can 
readjust your priorities

00:07:12.782 --> 00:07:15.100
and change direction.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:18.652
The US had universal child care once.

00:07:18.652 --> 00:07:20.398
And almost did again.

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:26.900
But with policy, it’s never really 
game over.

